>
  <section>
    <h2>The itch</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">I learn my choir part by ear while I work, and I made those practice tracks by hand.</p>
    <p>
      I sing tenor in a choir. The music arrives as a PDF from the choir director, and I learn my part by listening to
      practice tracks: my voice up front, the others behind it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Software that reads sheet music exists, but it makes mistakes, and the tools around it are complicated. To trust
      the result I would have had to check every bar against the original myself.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image
        src={checkSchumann}
        alt="Correction screen for bar 26, Tenor I, of Schumann’s “An die Sterne”: on the left the printed original with the bar and note in brackets and the page as a thumbnail, on the right the transcription with the tenor highlighted in yellow, the note in brackets, the note tool beside it and the question whether the bracketed note is right."
        widths={[800, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
      />
      <figcaption>Checking one uncertain note in Schumann’s “An die Sterne”: the printed original left, the transcription right. German UI.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Two readers check each other, and I only answer for my own voice.</p>
    <p>
      I drop the PDF into the folder. Audiveris, which is rule-based, and homr, a neural network, transcribe it
      independently. I pick my voice, and the app asks only about the notes of that voice where the two are unsure or
      disagree: the same passage from the PDF enlarged next to the transcription, the note in question bracketed in
      both. I answer “yes, that’s right” or correct the note where it stands.
    </p>
    <p>
      The export is MusicXML for MuseScore and a practice MP3 in about five seconds: my voice on harmonica, the others
      on flute, clarinet and horn, all equally loud. I asked for that on purpose: no louder own voice. That is where
      the name comes from: colla parte, in music, means an instrument plays along with the voice.
    </p>
    <div class="c-pair">
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={toolParry}
          alt="Correction screen for Parry’s “Crossing the Bar”: a small dark toolbar under the selected note offers higher, lower, accidentals and note values, and the panel below says nothing is open in the tenor any more."
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 45vw"
        />
        <figcaption>The tool opens at the note you click. Parry’s “Crossing the Bar”, every question answered. German UI.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={exportDialog}
          alt="Export dialog for a Bach chorale: a practice MP3 for the tenor at tempo 80, the opening of the original, flute, clarinet and horn for the other voices, harmonica for mine, and MusicXML."
          widths={[800, 1600]}
          sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 45vw"
        />
        <figcaption>The export: one instrument per voice, harmonica for mine. German UI.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Check, don’t retype</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Most voices need one to three answers, not a check of every bar.</p>
    <p>
      In Schumann’s “An die Sterne”, 83 bars for eight voices, most voices had one to three open questions. One had 22,
      because a whole line had been read an octave off. To see whether I could trust that, I checked all 57 bars of four
      voices of Poulenc’s “Salve Regina” by hand: no error in pitch, duration or accidentals slipped through unnoticed,
      and every real error had been asked as a question.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="noten-questions" ratio="1200 / 580" narrow="600 / 900" />
      <figcaption>Schumann’s “An die Sterne”, 83 bars for eight voices: most voices had one to three open questions, one had 22, because a whole line had been read an octave off.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <dl class="c-numbers">
      <div>
        <dt>9 days</dt>
        <dd>from a script to a Mac app with a redesign</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>109</dt>
        <dd>automated tests, about 12,400 lines</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>7 pieces</dt>
        <dd>tested, from the Renaissance to today</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>6 / 6</dt>
        <dd>pieces with every bar found on the PDF page</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The pieces range from Victoria’s double-choir “Ave Maria” (eight voices, 92 bars) and a Mundy piece with organ to
      Parry, Schumann, Poulenc, Whitacre’s “Sleep” with divided voices and a Bach chorale. Reading takes about a minute
      per page.
    </p>
    <ol class="c-timeline">
      <li><time>18 Oct 2026</time><span>A script reads the first PDF.</span></li>
      <li><time>20 Oct 2026</time><span>A real Mac app with its own window.</span></li>
      <li><time>26 Oct 2026</time><span>The redesign as a choir folder.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From “looks like AI” to a choir folder</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">The first interface looked like every AI-built app, so I had three directions drawn up.</p>
    <p>
      An audit named the patterns: blue pill buttons, identical cards with soft shadows, a dashed “+ New” tile, status
      dots. The three directions were a piano score, a choir folder with pencil, and plain Mac-native. I chose the choir
      folder: a black frame with white sheets inside. Pencil grey means “still to check”, highlighter yellow only “my
      voice”, red pencil only real errors, and there is no blue at all. The type is Edwin, in the style of Century
      Schoolbook, with music symbols from Bravura.
    </p>
    <figure>
