  <section>
    <h2>Details that took rounds</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">The mark, the screen and the zoom each went through choices before they stuck.</p>
    <p>
      I turned down a pencil circle around an uncertain note and tested three marks instead: grey like a pencil sketch,
      brackets like urtext editions, and a question mark. Brackets won. For the correction screen there were three
      layouts; the open folder won, and the tool appears right at the note instead of as a wall of buttons.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image
        src={marks}
        alt="Three ways to mark an uncertain note on a highlighted line: drawn in grey pencil, in square brackets, or with a small question mark above it."
        widths={[800, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
      />
      <figcaption>Three marks for an uncertain note. Brackets won, the way urtext editions mark doubtful notes. German UI.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image
        src={layoutFolder}
        alt="Layout one: the printed page on the left, the transcription on the right with the question underneath, like an open folder."
        widths={[800, 1200]}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
      />
      <figcaption>Layout one, chosen: the open folder, original left, transcription and question right. German UI.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="c-pair">
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={layoutMargin}
          alt="Layout two: the original above the transcription, with the question as a note in the right margin."
          widths={[600, 1200]}
          sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 45vw"
        />
        <figcaption>Proofreading with a margin note.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={layoutBar}
          alt="Layout three: one bar of the original and of the transcription, both very large, with the question above."
          widths={[600, 1200]}
          sizes="(max-width: 640px) 100vw, 45vw"
        />
        <figcaption>One bar, large. Bach chorale, German UI.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p>
      After the first real use I changed two things: the whole PDF page was too small to compare, so the same passage now
      sits enlarged next to the transcription, and the mark sometimes sat beside the note, so it now snaps to the printed
      note head.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Built with Claude Code</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">My part was the problem, the decisions and the testing; Claude Code wrote the code with me.</p>
    <p>
      I brought the problem, the requirements, the tests with real pieces, every design decision and the feedback rounds.
      The code was written with Claude Code as an AI pair programmer: Python with a local server in its own Mac window
      (pywebview), Audiveris and homr for reading, music21 and verovio for engraving, PyMuPDF for the PDF and MuseScore 4
      for the MP3. The interface is plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Everything runs on the Mac; nothing goes to a cloud.
    </p>
  </section>
